\chapter{Lingo Frontend Language}

This chapter describes the Lingo frontend language as implemented in the EightBol compiler's Lingo lexer and parser. The language is a subset of Lingo tailored for retro game development on 8-bit and 16-bit platforms.

@section Lexical Structure

Lingo source code is composed of tokens derived from the character stream. The lexer recognizes whitespace, comments, identifiers, keywords, literals, and operators.

@subsection Comments
Comments begin with a semicolon (@code{;}) or an asterisk (@code{*}) at the start of a line (after trimming leading whitespace) and extend to the end of the line. Comments are ignored by the lexer.

@subsection Identifiers
Identifiers in Lingo follow PascalCase or camelCase conventions. The lexer accepts alphanumeric characters, underscores (@code{_}), dollar signs (@code{$}), and hyphens (@code{-}).

All identifiers are normalized to Header-Case (Title-Case-With-Hyphens) form during AST generation. This ensures consistent naming across the compiled code regardless of the input source format.

@example
myVariable       @result My-Variable
PlayerHealth     @result Player-Health
_InternalName    @result Internal-Name
HTTPServer       @result HTTP-Server
@end example

@subsection Keywords (Reserved Words)
The following tokens are reserved as keywords and cannot be used as identifiers:

@multitable {Column Fractions} .33 .33 .33
@item ON @tab END @tab IF
@item THEN @tab ELSE @tab "END IF"
@item REPEAT @tab WITH @tab FOR
@item IN @tab TO @tab STEP
@item UP @tab DOWN @tab WHERE
@item WHILE @tab UNTIL @tab DO
@item LOOP @tab EXIT @tab NEXT
@item PUT @tab GET @tab SET
@item CALL @tab GOTO @tab GO
@item FRAME @tab PROPERTIES @tab PROPERTY
@item TELL @tab "END TELL" @tab METHOD
@item PROCEDURE @tab CLASS @tab RETURN
@item PLAY @tab STOP @tab HALT
@item AND @tab OR @tab NOT
@item TRUE @tab FALSE @tab NULL
@item SELF @tab ASSEMBLY @tab ENTRY
@item COPY @tab AS @tab DATA
@end multitable

Note: Multi-word keywords such as @code{END IF} and @code{END TELL} are recognized as single tokens during lexing.

@subsection Literals
Lingo supports numeric and string literals.

@subsubsection Integer Literals
Integer literals support multiple bases:

@subsubsubsection Decimal Literals
Decimal (base 10) literals consist of one or more decimal digits:

@example
42
0
123
@end example

@subsubsubsection Hexadecimal Literals
Hexadecimal (base 16) literals are prefixed with @code{0x}, @code{0X}, or @code{$}:

@example
0x1A
0xFF
$1F2E
@end example

@subsubsubsection Octal Literals
Octal (base 8) literals are prefixed with @code{0o} or @code{0O}:

@example
0o77
0O755
@end example

@subsubsubsection Binary Literals
Binary (base 2) literals are prefixed with @code{0b}, @code{0B}, or @code{%}:

@example
0b1010
0B11110000
%10101010
@end example

@subsubsubsection DWORD Literals
DWORD (32-bit double-word) literals are prefixed with @code{0d}:

@example
0d12345678
0d4294967295
@end example

@subsubsection String Literals
String literals are enclosed in double quotes (@code{"}). They may contain any sequence of characters except the double quote itself. Escape sequences are not supported.

@example
"Hello, world!"
""
"Level 1"
@end example

@subsection Operators
Lingo defines the following operators for use in expressions (though note: the current parser implementation does not fully support arithmetic or logical expressions in all contexts):

@multitable {Column Fractions} .33 .33 .33
@item + @tab - @tab *
@item / @tab = @tab ==
@item <> @tab != @tab <
@item > @tab <= @tab >=
@item : @tab , @tab (
@item ) @tab { @tab }
@item [ @tab ] @tab . 
@item ::= 
@end multitable

@subsection Expressions
Expressions combine literals, identifiers, and operators to produce values. However, the current frontend parser only supports a limited subset of expressions:

@subsubsection Identifier Expressions
A bare identifier evaluates to the value of the variable or property with that name.

@example
playerScore
myObject
@end example

@subsubsection Method Call Expressions
Objects can be invoked using the colon (@code{:}) or dot (@code{.}) syntax, followed by a method name and optional argument list.

@example
sprite:update()
player.moveTo(100, 20)
score.display()
@end example

@section Definitions
Lingo programs are composed of definitions that declare methods, procedures, and classes.

@subsection Method Definition
A method definition declares a procedure that is associated with a class or object and can access instance state.

@onsyntax
METHOD ident LPAREN param-list? RPAREN statement-list END
@offsyntax

@example
METHOD updatePlayer()
  CALL move()
  CALL animate()
END
@end example

@subsection Procedure Definition
A procedure definition declares a standalone routine that does not belong to any class.

@onsyntax
PROCEDURE ident LPAREN param-list? RPAREN statement-list END
@offsyntax

@example
PROCEDURE main()
  SET score :TO 0
  WHILE playerLives > 0 DO
    CALL updateGame()
  END
END
@end example

@subsection Class Definition
A class definition declares a new object type with properties and methods.

@onsyntax
CLASS ident statement-list END
@offsyntax

@example
CLASS Player
  PROPERTY x y
  METHOD init()
    SET x :TO 0
    SET y :TO 0
  END
END
@end example

Note: The exact syntax for parameter lists and the use of keywords like END may vary; consult the parser for details.
@subsection Statements
Lingo programs consist of a sequence of statements. Each statement performs an action such as assignment, control flow, or method invocation.

@subsubsection Assignment Statements
Assignment statements store values in variables or properties.

@paragraph{SET Statement}
The @code{SET} statement assigns the value of an expression to a target identifier.

@onsyntax
SET ident :TO expression
@offsyntax

@example
SET score :TO 0
SET playerName :TO "Hero"
@end example

@paragraph{MOVE Statement}
The @code{MOVE} statement assigns the value of an expression to a target identifier, with the source and target reversed compared to @code{SET}.

@onsyntax
MOVE expression :TO ident
@offsyntax

@example
MOVE 0 :TO score
MOVE "Hero" :TO playerName
@end example

@subsubsection Property Statement
The @code{PROPERTY} statement declares a list of identifiers as properties (though its current implementation is limited).

@onsyntax
PROPERTY identifiers
@offsyntax

@example
PROPERTY x y z
@end example

@subsubsection Call Statement
The @code{CALL} statement invokes a method or function identified by an expression, with optional arguments.

@onsyntax
CALL expression LPAREN arg-list? RPAREN
@offsyntax

@example
CALL initialize()
CALL sprite:draw(10, 20)
CALL Math.max(a, b)
@end example

@subsubsection Loop Statements
Lingo provides two loop constructs: @code{FOR} and @code{WHILE}.

@paragraph{FOR Loop}
The @code{FOR} loop iterates over a numeric range with an optional step size.

@onsyntax
FOR ident :IN expression :TO expression :STEP expression DO statement-list END
@offsyntax

@example
FOR i :IN 1 :TO 10 :STEP 1 DO
  PUT i
END
@end example

@paragraph{WHILE Loop}
The @code{WHILE} loop repeats as long as a condition expression evaluates to true.

@onsyntax
WHILE expression DO statement-list END
@offsyntax

@example
WHILE player.isAlive DO
  CALL updateGame()
END
@end example

@subsubsection Conditional Statements
The @code{IF} statement conditionally executes one of two statement lists.

@onsyntax
IF expression THEN statement-list ELSE statement-list? END IF
@offsyntax

@example
IF playerScore > highScore THEN
  SET highScore :TO playerScore
ELSE
  PUT "Try again!"
END IF
@end example

@subsubsection Return Statement
The @code{RETURN} statement exits the current method, optionally returning a value.

@onsyntax
RETURN expression?
@offsyntax

@examples
RETURN
RETURN playerScore
@end examples

@subsubsection Goto Statement
The @code{GOTO} statement transfers control to a labeled position in the code.

@onsyntax
GOTO ident
@offsyntax

@example
GOTO startLoop
@end example

@section Sample Program
@example
PROCEDURE main()
  SET score :TO 0
  SET playerLives :TO 3

  WHILE playerLives > 0 DO
    IF score >= 1000 THEN
      CALL showMessage("Congratulations! You win!")
      RETURN
    END IF
    
    CALL updatePlayer()
    CALL renderScene()
    
    IF player.isHit THEN
      MOVE playerLives - 1 :TO playerLives
    END IF
  END

  CALL showMessage("Game Over. Final score: ")
  CALL showScore(score)
END
@end example

@subsection Identifier Normalization

All identifiers in Lingo programs are normalized to Header-Case (Title-Case-With-Hyphens) format when generating AST nodes. This normalization:

@itemize
@item Converts PascalCase (@code{PlayerHealth}) to Header-Case (@code{Player-Health})
@item Converts camelCase (@code{playerHealth}) to Header-Case (@code{Player-Health})
@item Preserves underscores where appropriate but capitalizes subsequent words
@item Handles acronyms correctly (e.g., @code{HTTPServer} @result{} @code{HTTP-Server})
@end itemize

Normalization occurs consistently across method names, procedure names, variable names, parameters, and labels. This ensures the generated assembly code uses a canonical naming convention independent of how identifiers were written in the source Lingo code.
